Article 19
(1) Social reintegration services as referred to in Article 12 paragraph (1) letter e, shall be implemented with the following activities:
a. return and/or pick-up of victims;
b. population administration document services or personal identity services;
c. alternative family;
d. scholarships and educational support facilities;
e. non-formal education;
f. skills training and economic enterprise/entrepreneurship training;
g. financial assistance and business capital;
h. mentoring/assistance for economic business/enterprise activities;
i. food and/or nutrition assistance for victims and their families;
j. provision of social protection programs and poverty alleviation programs; and
k. other forms of return and social reintegration services needed by victims.
(2) Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be provided to victims, families and people whose lives depend on victims with the aim that victims have the readiness and ability to live life in society.
(3) Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be carried out in an integrated manner with the Integrated Referral Service System for Social Protection and Poverty Alleviation and the Child Social Welfare Center.
(4) Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus in charge of social affairs, population administration and civil registration affairs, labor affairs, small and medium enterprise cooperative affairs, education affairs, health affairs, public works and spatial planning affairs, land affairs, women's empowerment and child protection affairs and other related affairs.

CHAPTER IX
OBLIGATIONS OF THE CITY REGIONAL GOVERNMENT
Article 30
(1) The obligations of the City Regional Government in the implementation of Women's Protection are as follows:
a. formulate policies and incorporate Women's Protection programs in regional development planning and budgeting;
b. provide complaint services, health services, social rehabilitation, assistance and law enforcement as well as repatriation and social reintegration for women victims;
c. establish shelter services and/or safe houses;
d. establish special mechanisms for integrated hospital-based services;
e. establish PPT;
f. establish the UPT for Women and Children Protection;
g. formulate standard operating procedures and service referral systems;
h. conduct prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women;
i. provide human resources and infrastructure facilities for Women's Protection;
j. increase support and develop women's protection service institutions owned by the City Regional Government and the community;
k. develop victim services based on communities, boarding educational institutions, basic educational institutions, religious institutions, and the business sector;
l. conduct capacity building;
m. conduct coordination and cooperation;
n. conduct empowerment to increase community participation;
o. develop an integrated and interconnected data and information system for women's protection;
p. conduct control, guidance and supervision of the implementation of women's protection services; and
q. provide awards to the community, community organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, boarding educational institutions, the business sector and other parties.
(2) Planning and budgeting for Women's Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus implementing supporting functions of Government Affairs and the Service (Dinas).

Article 8
(1) Prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women is carried out in the following fields:

a. education;
b. health;
c. religion;
d. security;
e. justice;
f. public works and spatial planning;
g. public housing and residential areas;
h. social affairs;
i. labor;
j. women's empowerment and child protection;
k. defense;
l. environment;
m. population administration and civil registration;
n. population control and family planning;
o. transportation;
p. communication and informatics;
q. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises;
r. youth and sports;
s. culture;
t. tourism;
u. trade and industry; and
v. other fields.

(2) Prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by:

a. developing communication media, information, education and public campaigns through media;
b. developing educational materials and curriculum in accordance with regional authority;
c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. building early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, boarding educational environments, and other public spaces;
e. implementing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
f. encouraging behavioral change of parents and society;
g. developing regional policies, schools, boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
h. establishing and developing cadres, communities, young people, students, Islamic students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, community leaders and religious leaders for prevention;
i. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
j. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable women groups;
k. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
l. increasing the capacity of City Regional Government, government agencies, educational institutions, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sector, service institutions, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, media, religious leaders, community leaders and communities;
m. conducting cooperation with religious institutions, community social institutions, service institutions, universities, media, youth organizations, religious leaders and community leaders; and
n. conducting socialization of laws and regulations.

(3) The fields and forms of prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) are implemented by agencies, regional apparatus, institutions, community institutions, organizations, communities, professional institutions that handle related affairs in accordance with their authority based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 18
(1) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 12 paragraph (1) letter d, shall be implemented with the following activities:
a. counseling;
b. assistance;
c. provision of language expert services, and certified translators;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. safe house;
f. shelter/temporary shelter;
g. strengthening based on family and/or group support;
h. mobility support for victims with disabilities;
i. provision of social assistance for victims; and
j. other forms of social rehabilitation services needed by victims.
(2) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, letter b, letter c, letter d, letter e and letter g, shall be the responsibility of the Service (Dinas).
(3) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, letter h, letter i and letter j, shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus in charge of social affairs.

CHAPTER VI
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 23
(1) The City Regional Government has the obligation to strengthen community participation in the implementation of women's protection.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out in the form of:
a. prevention;
b. services and referrals;
c. development of information data systems;
d. strengthening of service institutions;
e. research and policy advocacy;
f. control, guidance and supervision; and
g. financing.
(3) Strengthening of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), can be carried out through:
a. strengthening cooperation; and
b. strengthening institutional capacity for prevention and services from the community, boarding educational institutions, and the business sector.
(4) The business sector has responsibility in the implementation of Women's Protection in accordance with the targets of Corporate Social and Environmental Responsibility.
(5) The form of business sector responsibility as referred to in paragraph (4), is carried out as follows:
a. financial assistance to service institutions;
b. empowerment;
c. capacity building;
d. publication and information media;
e. provision of facilities and infrastructure;
f. establishment of special institutions for prevention and services for violence complaints; and
g. non-binding social assistance.

Article 4
The scope of regulation for the implementation of Women's Protection includes:
a. women's rights;
b. prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women;
c. services;
d. coordination and cooperation;
e. community participation;
f. data and information systems;
g. institutional matters;
h. obligations of the City Regional Government;
i. control, guidance and supervision;
j. awards; and
k. financing.

Article 15
(1) Health rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 12 paragraph (1) letter b, shall be adjusted to the needs of victims based on medical and non-medical considerations, consisting of:

a. physical and mental health examination and treatment;
b. psychological examination and recovery;
c. pregnancy, childbirth, and reproductive health services;
d. medical resume and psychological examination results;
e. visum et repertum and visum et psikiatrikum;
f. deoxyribonucleic acid test; and
g. other forms of health services needed.

(2) Health rehabilitation services for female victims shall be conducted by trained officers in a special room that is safe and comfortable.

(3) Health rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be conducted through a special mechanism of integrated services based on hospitals and shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus with authority in the field.
